---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../data/site";
import { c2 } from "../data/c2_results";
const f2 = (x: number) => x.toFixed(2);
const iv = (v: number[]) => `${f2(v[1])} to ${f2(v[2])}`;
const pc = (x: number) => Math.round(x * 100);
const usd = (x: number) => `$${Math.round(x)}`;
const gainPct = Math.round((c2.asreml_ate[0] / c2.mean_main_yield) * 100);
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
  name: "Study C2: does wheat sown earlier yield more?",
  description: "Same-paddock pairs of Early and Main National Variety Trials in NSW and Queensland, 2008 to 2017: the same variety yielded about 0.2 t/ha more when sown in the Early window.",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Max Moldovan", identifier: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9680-8474" },
  datePublished: "2026-10-13",
};
const rows = [
  { label: "All pairs", n: c2.n_pairs, a: c2.asreml_ate, b: c2.fb_ate, p: c2.pm_ate, s: c2.apsim_ate },
  { label: "Southern NSW", n: c2.n_pairs_south, a: c2.asreml_south, b: c2.fb_south, p: c2.pm_south, s: c2.apsim_south },
  { label: "North", n: c2.n_pairs_north, a: c2.asreml_north, b: c2.fb_north, p: c2.pm_north, s: c2.apsim_north },
];
const cell = (v: number[]) => (Number.isFinite(v[0]) ? `${f2(v[0])} (${iv(v)})` : "not run");
---
<Base title="Sowing time" description="Study C2, on real trial data: in same-paddock pairs of Early and Main National Variety Trials in NSW and Queensland, 2008 to 2017, the same wheat variety yielded about 0.2 t/ha more when sown in the Early window. How the packages worked together, what the gain is worth, and what the data cannot rule out." path="/c2" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">Study C2 · real trial data · 2026-10-13</p>
      <h1>Does wheat sown earlier yield more?</h1>
      <p class="prose lead mt-2">Some National Variety Trial (NVT) sites sow wheat twice in a season, in
        two trials side by side: an Early series ahead of the usual sowing window and the Main series
        within it. Because both trials share the paddock, the season and the varieties, comparing them
        shows what an earlier sowing date does to yield with most other influences held fixed.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The data</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>The pairs.</strong> An Early and a Main trial of the same season on the same paddock
        form a pair. The pairs were fixed before any yield was read: {c2.n_pairs} pairs at {c2.n_sites}{" "}
        sites in NSW and Queensland, {c2.year_min} to {c2.year_max}, sown {c2.gap_min_days} to
        {" "}{c2.gap_max_days} days apart (median {c2.gap_median_days}).</li>
      <li><strong>The comparison.</strong> Each variety grown in both trials of a pair gives one
        difference, Early yield minus Main yield: {c2.n_variety_pairs.toLocaleString("en-AU")} differences
        across {c2.n_varieties} varieties.</li>
      <li><strong>The source.</strong> The open NVT results compiled by Newman and Furbank (2021), read
        through DataHub, with soil, rainfall and fertiliser records for each trial. Sites are split into
        southern NSW and the north (northern NSW and Queensland) at 31.5° S.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">How it was analysed</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>Did the trials match before sowing?</strong> kernR compared Early and Main trials on
        soil (clay, pH, water-holding capacity) and on the nitrogen applied on or before the sowing day,
        all at once.
        It found no difference (permutation p = {f2(c2.kernr_p_primary)}). The month before sowing was slightly wetter
        for Early trials ({Math.round(c2.rain_early_mm)} against {Math.round(c2.rain_main_mm)} mm), as
        expected from the earlier date; with rainfall added the test still finds no difference
        (p = {f2(c2.kernr_p_with_rain)}).</li>
      <li><strong>The main estimate.</strong> ASReml and flexyBayes fit the same mixed model to the
        within-pair differences, with site, year, variety and pair as random effects. Comparing within a
        pair already holds site, season and variety fixed, so these models use no other covariates.
        flexyBayes also gives the full posterior that the decision step uses.</li>
      <li><strong>A check without the pairing.</strong> proxymix, a mixture model that does not assume
        normal errors, estimates the gain from the same rows without using the pairs. It adjusts instead
        for where the site is, its soil, the rain before sowing and the nitrogen applied on or before the sowing day.</li>
      <li><strong>What the crop model expects.</strong> apsimx wrote each site's weather and simulation
        files and ran APSIM at both sowing dates. PESTO calibrated APSIM's starting soil water and nitrogen
        to each pair's site.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparing the analyses.</strong> quorum compared the estimates: whether they agree on the
        size of the gain and on how uncertain it is.</li>
      <li><strong>What it is worth.</strong> decideR turned the flexyBayes posterior and the wheat price of
        the study seasons into a value per hectare and a choice between the two sowing windows.</li>
      <li><strong>One plan, run blind first.</strong> conductoR ran the main analysis as one plan, written
        in a YAML file, and logged each step. The plan ran first on a table in which the Early and Main
        labels of a random half of the pairs had been swapped, was then frozen, and ran once on the real
        labels.</li>
      <li><strong>A known answer first.</strong> Before either run, each analysis had to recover a known
        effect ({f2(c2.pc_truth)} t/ha) from a copy of the same design with yields simulated by APSIM. All
        three did.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Three numbers</h2>
    <div class="studies">
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">The average gain</p>
        <h3>{f2(c2.asreml_ate[0])} t/ha ({iv(c2.asreml_ate)})</h3>
        <p class="prose">On the same paddock and in the same season, the same variety yielded
          {" "}{f2(c2.asreml_ate[0])} t/ha more sown in the Early window, about {gainPct} per cent of a
          mean Main-window yield of {c2.mean_main_yield.toFixed(1)} t/ha (ASReml). flexyBayes gives
          {" "}{f2(c2.fb_ate[0])}{Number.isFinite(c2.pm_ate[0]) && <> and proxymix {f2(c2.pm_ate[0])}</>}.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">What it is worth</p>
        <h3>{usd(c2.value_ate_aud_ha[0])}/ha ({usd(c2.value_ate_aud_ha[1])} to {usd(c2.value_ate_aud_ha[2])})</h3>
        <p class="prose">The flexyBayes gain valued at {usd(c2.price_aud_t)}/t, the ABARES wheat gross unit
          value (the value of production per tonne) averaged over the ten study seasons ({usd(c2.price_low_aud_t)} to {usd(c2.price_high_aud_t)}/t
          across them), with early sowing taken to cost nothing extra. The range covers yield uncertainty
          only. The chance that the average gain is positive is {pc(c2.p_gain)} per cent, and decideR
          chooses the Early window.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <p class="kicker">On any one paddock</p>
        <h3>{pc(c2.p_gain_new_paddock)} per cent chance of a gain</h3>
        <p class="prose">The gain varies widely between sites and seasons. On a new paddock and season the
          expected value of sowing early is similar, {usd(c2.decider_value_new_paddock_aud_ha)}/ha in
          decideR, but the chance of any gain at all falls to about two in three.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-3">The estimates</h2>
    <p class="prose">Early minus Main yield, t/ha. In brackets, the 90 per cent interval: a confidence
      interval for ASReml, a credible interval for flexyBayes, a bootstrap interval for proxymix and the
      spread of the calibrated simulations for APSIM.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll mt-1">
      <table class="num">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Pairs</th><th scope="col">n</th><th scope="col">ASReml</th><th scope="col">flexyBayes</th><th scope="col">proxymix</th><th scope="col">APSIM with PESTO</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{r.label}</th><td>{r.n}</td><td>{cell(r.a)}</td><td>{cell(r.b)}</td><td>{cell(r.p)}</td><td>{cell(r.s)}</td></tr>
          ))}
          <tr><th scope="row">Sown under a week apart</th><td>{c2.n_pairs_same_week}</td><td>{cell(c2.asreml_same_week)}</td><td>{cell(c2.fb_same_week)}</td><td>not estimated</td><td>not estimated</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="prose mt-2">
      <li>The gain is clearest in southern NSW. In the north the ASReml and flexyBayes intervals include
        zero, so a gain there is not established.</li>
      <li>APSIM, calibrated with PESTO, expected less than the trials show. Every ASReml and flexyBayes
        interval still overlaps the APSIM range.</li>
      <li>quorum reports the analyses as agreeing on where the gain lies but not on how uncertain it is.
        proxymix's interval is much narrower, most likely because it does not carry the variation between
        sites and seasons that the mixed models include.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">Nitrogen applied by sowing day</h2>
    <p class="prose">About half of the nitrogen applied to these trials goes on after sowing, as an in-crop
      top-dressing. On average, Early trials received {Math.round(c2.n_total_early)} kg N/ha, of which
      {" "}{Math.round(c2.n_sow_early)} was applied on or before the sowing day; Main trials received
      {" "}{Math.round(c2.n_total_main)}, of which {Math.round(c2.n_sow_main)}. How much nitrogen goes on
      in-crop can depend on how the crop looks, and so on when it was sown, so it cannot be used to check
      that the trials matched before sowing. The balance check and the proxymix covariates therefore use
      only the nitrogen applied on or before the sowing day. The ASReml and flexyBayes
      estimates use no covariates and are unchanged.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-3">What it does not show</h2>
    <ul class="prose">
      <li><strong>Trials with no yields.</strong> A trial with no yields in the data cannot be paired, and
        the data do not say why. Of {c2.lost_candidates} same-paddock pairs sown a week or more apart,
        {" "}{c2.lost_early_only} lost only the Early trial's yields, {c2.lost_main_only} only the Main
        trial's and {c2.lost_both} both. Early trials lost their yields more often. If those crops failed,
        leaving them out raises the Early average, and no adjustment removes that.</li>
      <li><strong>More than sowing date.</strong> Pairs sown under a week apart still differed by
        {" "}{f2(c2.asreml_same_week[0])} t/ha ({iv(c2.asreml_same_week)}), so part of the gap may come
        from the trial series rather than the sowing date. These pairs sit at different sites from the
        rest, so the figure is reported beside the main estimate and not subtracted from it.</li>
      <li><strong>Where on the paddock.</strong> In the causal graph of the design (Hernán and Robins
        2020), a pair shares site, season and variety, so comparing within it adjusts for them. Anything
        else that differs between the Early and Main trials is not adjusted for. The estimate assumes the
        Early trials were not sown on a better or worse part of the paddock than the Main trials.</li>
      <li><strong>The price.</strong> The value assumes early sowing costs nothing extra and uses the
        average price of the study seasons, weighted by the number of comparisons in each, not a forecast.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-3">References</h2>
    <ul class="prose refs">
      <li>ABARES (2026). <em>Agricultural commodities: September quarter 2026</em>, statistical tables,
        Table 6. Australian Bureau of Agricultural and Resource Economics and Sciences.</li>
      <li>Hernán, M. A., &amp; Robins, J. M. (2020). <em>Causal inference: What if</em>. Chapman &amp;
        Hall/CRC.</li>
      <li>Newman, S. J., &amp; Furbank, R. T. (2021). A multiple species, continent-wide, million-phenotype
        agronomic plant dataset. <em>Scientific Data</em>, 8, 116.
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-00898-8">https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-00898-8</a>
        (author correction: <em>Scientific Data</em>, 9, 437, 2022).</li>
      <li>Sparks, A. (2026). <em>read.abares: Read data from ABARES and ABS</em> (R package version 3.0.0).
        <a href="https://docs.ropensci.org/read.abares/">https://docs.ropensci.org/read.abares/</a></li>
      <li>The VSNi Team (2026). <em>asreml: Fits linear mixed models using REML</em> (R package version
        4.2.0.482). VSN International.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/a0")}>The simulated study</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/members")}>The members</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/about")}>About</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
